{
  "findings": [
    {
      "id": "signal-and-broadcast",
      "text": "With several waiters registered on the same Cond, one Signal makes one waiter eligible to resume. A second Signal can wake another remaining waiter, and a subsequent Broadcast wakes all remaining waiters. Each awakened waiter must reacquire c.L before returning. The notifier may call either method with or without holding c.L.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 75,
        "fragment": "Signal wakes one goroutine waiting on c, if there is any."
      },
      "question": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "notification-order",
      "text": "If Signal or Broadcast completes while nobody is registered, a subsequent Wait registers a new ticket and needs a later notification. If Wait registers its ticket first and a notification covers that ticket before the goroutine parks, the notification still counts and Wait proceeds to reacquire c.L.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 69,
        "fragment": "t := runtime_notifyListAdd(&c.notify)"
      },
      "question": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "predicate-after-wakeup",
      "text": "Start with a caller holding c.L and observing a false predicate. Wait registers the caller and releases c.L. Another goroutine can acquire c.L, make the predicate true and Signal. A competing goroutine can then acquire c.L before the waiter and make the predicate false again. The waiter returns with c.L held and must recheck the predicate before proceeding.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 81,
        "fragment": "they may be awoken before a \"waiting\" goroutine."
      },
      "question": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "cancellation",
      "text": "Once Wait has registered and released c.L, expiration of an external deadline or cancellation of an external context does not wake it. Wait takes no cancellation input and has no cancellation return path. A Signal or Broadcast that wakes the waiter allows it to continue, and it still waits to reacquire c.L before returning.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 55,
        "fragment": "Wait cannot return unless awoken by [Cond.Broadcast] or [Cond.Signal]."
      },
      "question": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "copy-identity",
      "text": "After NewCond assigns the supplied Locker, a value copy made before any Wait, Signal or Broadcast has its own notification list and zero-valued checker while retaining the Locker value. Each instance initializes its checker to its own address on first use. Concurrent first calls on the same instance can race to initialize it, and the losing call accepts the matching address on recheck. If a value copy is made after any of those methods initializes the checker, the first such call on the copy panics before touching its notification list or unlocking c.L.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 107,
        "fragment": "!atomic.CompareAndSwapUintptr((*uintptr)(c), 0, uintptr(unsafe.Pointer(c))) &&"
      },
      "question": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "broadcast-channel-analogy",
      "text": "If Broadcast wakes the current waiters and a new caller enters Wait afterward, the new caller needs another notification. If a channel is closed and a new receiver arrives afterward, the receive can complete immediately. Repeated Broadcast calls can wake successive groups of waiters, while closing an already closed channel panics. The documented synchronizes-before guarantee applies to Wait calls that the notification unblocks.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 27,
        "fragment": "Broadcast corresponds to closing a channel"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Is the analogy meant to cover only the wakeup of goroutines already waiting when the event occurs?"
    }
  ]
}
